rota lap 4587, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; advanced by hand past row 2, which my own previous lap read. Taste only works up close, so the row's instruction is to run the actual thing rather than read the sentence about it. That is what turned a ledger row I could have argued about into four readings taken on live state, and it is also what found the second fault: the scan's header said its unheld reading was "printed row by row" and the code printed a count.
think Reds first, and the fleet could book none. `reds_pin_capacity_scan.sh` read `pin_headroom=22`, `pin_open_rows=16`, `pin_foldable_rows=0`, `pin_deadlocked=1` -- no lawful fold, no room for a row. My own previous lap built that instrument, and its `pin_deadlock_doors` line named the first door as the fleet's own rather than Keaton's: 7 of 16 open rows name no hand outside the loop, and closing one makes `reds_fold.sh` lawful in the same lap.
think Picked `%457` of the seven. Its repair, `tools/fixtures/p/path_absence_scan.sh`, landed `20260906` and was proven then; the row stayed OPEN on its remainder, that nothing makes a lap consult it before concluding a file is gone.
think Re-proved the repair on metal rather than citing its elder proof, and the tree handed me a live case: `git fetch xy` read `commits_behind=1` mid-lap. Four readings on that live divergence -- a path both sides hold, a second path both sides hold, a fabricated name reading `verdict=absent`, and `tools/t/tilak_root_count_witness.rish`, a witness the anointed spine carried and this checkout did not, reading `verdict=behind` by name.
think The remainder is a habit, and a lap's own grep sits in no file. That is the same structural wall `%512` met for a tracked-source scan, `%549` for a shell redirect and `%569` for a signal: no instrument this tree can own reads a command typed at a session prompt. The tree's settled answer for that class is the baton.
think Measured the reach before writing the clause: `path_absence_scan.sh` stood on ZERO living directive surfaces four days after landing -- `tools/f/fleet_baton.txt` 0, `construction/ITINERARY.md` 0, `.claude/rules/` and `.cursor/rules/` 0, `recursion-prompts/` 0, `expanding-prompts/` 0, `construction/standing-equipment.kyri` 0. `%568`'s lesson in a second room: a repair nothing points at is a repair nobody reaches for.
think Then read the scan's own header and found it claiming what the code did not do. `pin_unheld_rows` was printed as a count, its one naming sentence rode inside the DEADLOCK branch, and the header written six hours earlier said "printed row by row." A count says a door exists; a list says which door -- and the naming vanished exactly when the pin was healthy enough for a lap to act on cheaply.
obs A number that names no row cannot be acted on, and a number the header claims is a list is worse than either, because a reader stops looking. The repair is four lines of awk and a loop; what made it findable was reading the file the row was about instead of the sentence about it.
obs Two readers of one truth, held to each other rather than assumed: `PIN_UNHELD` stays derived as `PIN_OPEN - PIN_HELD`, `pin_unheld_named` counts the enumeration, and a control leg asserts they agree. The elder disagreement between `pin_held_rows` and the doors line already cost one morning and four ships.
obs Mutation-proven both ways before I believed the legs: dropping the awk's `else` branch reds three legs, and printing every open row unconditionally reds three others. A refusal proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass.
obs What I did not take: the cadence clock. `cadence_never_run_here=74` on this pier, up from 58 when `%568` booked it three days ago, and 0 of 74 hold a receipt. A drip at a bounded rate is buildable and the reds it would surface have nowhere in the pin to go until the bound moves, so turning it on is a cost decision that stays Keaton's.
